        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "a1ef6751-cc85-5e98-b3ec-a1a60f648c04",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Bolesław Prus, Otto von Bismarck\n- Works: The Outpost (Placówka)\n- Places: Poland, Russia, Prussia, Austria, Germany\n- Topics: rural poverty, colonization, Partition of Poland\n\nFacts:\n- Otto von Bismarck was born on 1 April 1815 and died on 30 July 1898.\n- Otto von Bismarck was a conservative Prussian statesman who dominated German and European affairs from the 1860s until 1890.\n- Poland had been partitioned about a century before Bolesław Prus wrote The Outpost, by Russia, Prussia, and Austria.\n- The Outpost, whose Polish title is Placówka, was the first of four major novels by the Polish writer Bolesław Prus.\n- In The Outpost, Bolesław Prus sought to draw attention to the plight of rural Poland.\n- Rural Poland in The Outpost is described as contending with poverty, ignorance, neglect by the upper crust, and colonization by German settlers backed by Otto von Bismarck's German government.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
